<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp2sec5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

    <title>Subspaces and Set Operations</title>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-ss-intersections_two">
        <title>The intersection of two subspaces is a subspace</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>W_1,W_2\subset V</m> are vector subspaces, then <m>W_1\cap W_2</m> is a vector subspace.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a direct proof.
            Allow the definitions to structure your proof.
            This is what we call a <q>follow your nose</q> proof, in the sense there is only really one, straightforward, way to go.
            You may find <xref ref="def-subspace"/>, Proposition <xref ref="prop-subspaces_contain_zero"/>, and <xref ref="def-set_ops"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <proposition>
        <title>The intersection of arbitrarily many subspaces is a subspace</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\{W_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I}</m> is a (possibly infinite) collection of subspaces of <m>V</m>, then their intersection <m>\cap_{\alpha\in I} W_\alpha</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a direct proof.
            Generalize your proof of Proposition <xref ref="prop-ss-intersections_two"/>.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <definition xml:id="def-subspace_sums">
        <title>Subspace Sum</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>V</m> is a vector space and <m>W_1,W_2\subset V</m> are subspaces, then their <term>sum</term> is the set
            </p>
            <me>W_1+W_2:= \{ \vect{w}_1+\vect{w}_2\ | \ \vect{w}_1\in W_1, \vect{w}_2\in W_2\}.</me>
            <p>
                More generally, the <term>sum</term> of a finite collection of subspaces <m>W_1,\ldots, W_r\subset V</m> is
            </p>
            <me>W_1+W_2+\cdots+W_r:= \{ \vect{w}_1+\vect{w}_2+\cdots + \vect{w}_r\ | \ \vect{w}_1\in W_1, \vect{w}_2\in W_2, \ldots, \vect{w}_r\in W_r \}.</me>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
        Observe how we are implicitly using associativity in the definition of <m>W_1+W_2+\cdots+W_r</m> when <m>r>2</m>.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-ss-sums">
        <title>The sum of two subspaces is a subspace</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>W_1,W_2\subset V</m> are vector subspaces, then their sum <m>W_1+ W_2</m> is a vector subspace.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a direct proof.
            Allow the definitions to structure your proof.
            This is another <q>follow your nose</q> proof.
            You may find <xref ref="def-subspace"/>, Proposition <xref ref="prop-subspaces_contain_zero"/>, and <xref ref="def-subspace_sums"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <corollary>
        <title>The sum of finitely many subspaces is a subspace</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>W_1,W_2, \ldots W_r\subset V</m> are vector subspaces, then <m>W_1+ W_2+\cdots W_r</m> is a vector subspace.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try an inductive proof.
            Observe <m>W_1+ W_2+\cdots W_r = (W_1+ W_2+\cdots W_{r-1})+W_r</m> (WHY?)
            You may find <xref ref="def-subspace_sums"/>, <xref ref="def-vectorspace"/>, and Proposition <xref ref="prop-ss-sums"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <insight>
    <p>
        If <m>W_1,W_2\subset V</m> are vector subspaces, then their union <m>W_1\cup W_2</m> is almost never a vector subspace.
        Carefully demonstrate this in <m>\R^2</m> visually with lines and symbolically with subspaces <m>W_1</m> and <m>W_2</m> as defined in Proposition <xref ref="prop-ss_zero_in_one"/>.
    </p>
    </insight>

   

</section>








